Those who lurk first are always welcome, since they are following the
good convention to first listen, then participate.

> This happened on the very first dungeon level to a lawful male human
> samurai named after myself: I thought I should #twoweapon from the
> very beginning for practice, so I did. Unfortunately I found out I was
> really clumsy and missed everything.

To effectively twoweapon, you should first be proficient in the
individual weapons: to determine your skill at twoweaponing, the lesser
one of twoweaponing and the individual weapon skill(s) is used. So, it
is better to first (onehandedly) raise your skill in the katana, then in
the other weapon you want to use, and only then in twoweaponing.

> A couple of rats and a jackal
> caught me unaware, bit me hard and made me #pray for my life. After
> killing them -on low HP again- I found an altar to Amaterasu Omikami
> and thought I was lucky. There was a wand over there, which might come
> in handy if it wasn't cursed.

A cursed wand is only *very* slightly worse than an uncursed or blessed
one. The only difference is a very slight chance of not working properly
(I'm not sure whether or not it will explode), but the wand's effects,
if it functions, are the same as for non-cursed wands.

> A newt entered the room and I -on 4HP or so- decided to try the wand
> on it, just in case. It happened to be a Wand of Polymorph, which
> turned the : into a blue a, which made me pray (without the #).

A lot of wands work in favour of monsters. I would never try one out
"just in case". Also, you had just prayed (in your jackal incident,
above), so you took a big risk praying quite soon afterward. Normally,
you would have been immobilised for the duration of the prayer, but
*not* invincible, as during a successful prayer. The newt/soldier ant
would have had ample opportunity to slaughter you.

> I considered myself already dead, but as a last resort, I tried the
> wand on myself, only to die instantly in an unsuccessful polymorph.

> Is that normal? Do I need some ability in order to polymorph myself
> with the wand? Does it have to do with my being in bad health?

Yes, no, a bit.

It is normal that polymorph is not always successful. I am not familiar
with the exact numbers, but IIRC you have a chance of resisting and
failing, and if the polymorph fails, you have to make a (D&D) saving
throw against system shock. The higher your constitution, the smaller
your chance of failing that save. If you fail the save, you die (or at
least, you lose a lot of HP, which, at low HP, is obviously lethat as
well). Anyway, you don't need a special ability to polymorph, but a high
constitution (and perhaps HP) helps in succeeding and surviving if you
fail.

Also, note that you cannot control your polymorphs: you might just as
well have turned into a newt yourself. Having and wearing a ring of
polymorph control lets you choose the form you turn into, *and*
eliminates the system shock effect.

